.TH VARCO-PRIVACY 8 "2026-11-03" "varco 1.0.3" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
varco-privacy \- reduce the identifiers this computer exposes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B varco-privacy
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Before any change the original values are written to a journal
(\fI/var/lib/varco-privacy/state.json\fR) atomically. Only then is the
system modified, so an interruption never loses them.
.B restore
undoes each change, verifies it, and forgets it only after the verification;
it can be run any number of times. Removing the package runs
.BR restore ;
if that fails the removal stops, unless
\fBVARCO_PRIVACY_FORCE_REMOVE=1\fR is set. Purging never deletes a
journal that still holds original values.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR status " [" \-\-json ]
Show what identifies this computer. Does not require root.
.TP
.B mac random\fR|\fBstable\fR|\fBoff
Random MAC address at every connection, or a fake address that stays the
same for each network (NetworkManager only), or the original address.
Without NetworkManager the change applies from the next restart.
Also enables random MAC addresses for Wi-Fi scans, IPv6 temporary addresses
and DHCP identifiers derived from the MAC address.
.TP
.B hostname \fR[\fIname\fR]
Use a generic computer name (default \fIdebian\fR).
.TP
.B machine-id
Generate a new
.IR /etc/machine-id .
A restart is needed.
.TP
.B hardware on\fR|\fBoff
Hide hardware serial numbers (DMI/SMBIOS, UUID, disk serials exposed in
\fI/sys\fR) behind generic values with bind mounts, also at every boot
(\fIvarco-mask.service\fR). The real values in the firmware are not
changed. Firmware update tools may stop working while this is on.
.TP
.B all
Everything above.
.TP
.B restore
Put every original value back.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/varco-privacy/state.json
Journal of the changes with the original values.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/network/99-default.link.d/varco-privacy.conf
MAC address policy when NetworkManager is not installed.
.TP
.I /etc/NetworkManager/conf.d/99-varco-privacy.conf
MAC address settings for NetworkManager.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR varco (1),
.BR machine-id (5),
.BR NetworkManager.conf (5)
